    "narrative": "With the scramble for stolen bases remaining our primary mandate, **Corbin Carroll** (66.31) and **Mickey Moniak** (69.76) anchor the lineup today. **Corbin Carroll** draws a favorable spot against Ranger Suarez; despite a minor platoon penalty, the aggressive nature of this matchup against a slow-delivering lefty boosts his outlook for a multi-steal game. **Mickey Moniak** carries the highest score of our starting group, buoyed significantly by a massive +14% park factor and a juicy matchup against Eric Lauer, though we must keep a close eye on the 37% rain risk in that stadium, which threatens to derail his production. **Isaac Paredes** (72.13) represents our strongest pure value play, as he gets a favorable look at George Klassen\u2019s 6.12 xERA. That matchup, combined with a +9% boost from dynamic platoon projections, makes **Isaac Paredes** the centerpiece of our offensive strategy today.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Josh Naylor**, who missed the cut with a 44.49 score. While he usually profiles as a reliable starter, a difficult matchup against Kyle Harrison\u2014coupled with a significant SP skill penalty\u2014pushed him below our efficiency threshold. The optimizer prioritized the upside of **Vinnie Pasquantino** (63.00), who benefits from a stronger platoon advantage and a higher-leverage spot in the order. Elsewhere, **Seiya Suzuki** (64.56) remains a high-floor play bolstered by favorable wind conditions, and **Austin Riley** (59.52) continues to get the nod due to a subtle +2% boost from a slow-delivering pitcher, which increases the likelihood of a stolen base despite his unconventional placement in the #8 hole. We are rolling with this configuration to maximize our efficiency while we continue to stash **James Wood** on the IL, pending further updates on his recovery.",
